   "title": "NM Historic Sites",
   "text": "Skip To Content\nEnglish\nABOUT\nSITES\nEVENTS\nNew Mexico Historic Sites\nare places that invite you to explore 600+ years of history and culture at some of the State's most amazing and unique locations. Come learn the untold stories of New Mexico and explore history where it happened.\nSearch\nUpcoming Events\nOctober 30, 2026\nGuided Tours of Taylor-Mesilla Historic Site\nTaylor-Mesilla Historic Site\nOctober 30, 2026\nFort Stanton Flashlight Tour - SOLD OUT!\nFort Stanton Historic Site\nVIEW ALL EVENTS\nVisit with a New Mexico CulturePass!\nNew Mexico CulturePass allows one visit to each historic site for only $30.\nLEARN MORE ABOUT THE CULTURE PASS\nExplore History Where It Happened\nBosque Redondo\nFort Sumner, NM\nA unique museum designed by Navajo architect David Sloan - shaped like a hogan and a tepee - and an interpretive trail, provide information about the tragic history of Fort Sumner and Bosque Redondo Indian Reservation.\nVISIT BOSQUE REDONDO\nCoronado\nBernalillo, NM\nLearn about the centuries-old history of the Pueblo village of Kuaua. By the time Spaniards entered the region in 1540CE, Kuaua had 1,200+ rooms built across 3 plazas with people practicing farming, pottery, and weaving. Today, history lives on as nearby Pueblos maintain ancestral ties to this site.\nVISIT CORONADO\nFort Selden\nRadium Springs, NM\nFort Selden preserves more than 1400 years of history beginning with the ancient Mogollon; travelers camping at the site on El Camino Real in the 17th century; the U.S. Army soldiers in the 1800s; to the local people who lived nearby. Their stories enrich the history and heritage of New Mexico.\nVISIT FORT SELDEN\nFort Stanton\nFort Stanton, NM\nFort Stanton is situated on 240 acres and surrounded by 25,000 acres of undeveloped BLM land in south-central New Mexico. There are 88 buildings on this historic site, some dating back to 1855.\nVISIT FORT STANTON\nJemez\nJemez Springs, NM\nA short drive from Albuquerque and Bernalillo, the Jemez National Historic Landmark is one of the most beautiful historic sites in the Southwest. It includes the stone remnants of a 700 year old village and the San José de los Jemez church dating to 1621/2.\nVISIT JEMEZ\nLincoln\nLincoln, NM\nA town made famous by one of the most violent periods in NM history. See the Old Courthouse with exhibits detailing the Lincoln County War. Walk in the footsteps of Billy the Kid, Pat Garrett, and other characters of the Wild West.\nVISIT LINCOLN\nLos Luceros\nAlcalde, NM\nFeaturing beautiful river views, ancient cottonwoods, 17th century buildings, apple orchards, farm animals and rolling agricultural fields, this property embodies the intersection of nature and culture in northern New Mexico. Travel back in time to a place that has been called home since the 1400s.\nVISIT LOS LUCEROS\nTaylor-Mesilla\nMesilla, NM\nLocated on the historic plaza in Old Mesilla, the Taylor-Mesilla Historic Site embodies the rich history and heritage of the Southwest Borderlands. The property is listed on the National Register of Historic Places and consists of two storefronts and a large residence.\nVISIT TAYLOR-MESILLA\nGet Updates and Event Announcements\nEmail\nNM Historic Sites\nLincoln and Fort Stanton\nCoronado and Jemez\nFort Selden\nBosque Redondo\nLos Luceros\nTaylor-Mesilla\nNMHS Staff\nSign up\nFollow Historic Sites on Social Media\nGreetings from Fort Selden! Did you know: Russian\nGreetings from Lincoln, This Saturday, Oct. 31, j\nEarlier this month, @fyiplus.nm joined Taylor-Mesi\nGreetings from Fort Stanton, During the late 1870\nFOLLOW\nFOLLOW\nFOLLOW\nUpcoming Historic Sites Events\nDiscover what’s happening across New Mexico’s vibrant cultural landscape. From museum exhibitions and historic site tours to live performances, workshops, and community celebrations, there’s always something to experience.\nVIEW ALL EVENTS\n30\nOCT 2026\nFort Stanton Flashlight Tour - SOLD OUT!\nFort Stanton Historic Site offers flashlight walking tours of the grounds after hours, allowing visitors a chance to explore areas generally closed to the public. Unfortunately, this tour on Fri., Oct. 30 is SOLD OUT. Catch us next summer and early fall for a new season!\nEVENT DETAILS\n31\nOCT 2026\n12th Annual Día de los Muertos Festival at Historic Lincoln\nLincoln community members are hosting the 12th annual town celebration of Día de los Muertos or Day of the Dead on Sat., Oct. 31, 10 a.m. - 3:30 p.m. and Lincoln Historic Site is participating!\nEVENT DETAILS\n1\nNOV 2026\nDawn 'Til Dusk at Los Luceros\nLos Luceros Historic Site offers extended hours from sunrise to sunset on the first Sunday of each month. Join us on Sun., Nov. 1, 6:30 a.m. - 5 p.m. Admission is FREE for New Mexico residents with ID.\nEVENT DETAILS\nLatest News\nStay connected to the stories of New Mexico Historic Sites\nVIEW ALL NEWS\nRELEASE: Fantastical folk-art Alebrijes: A Linares Family Tradition exhibit coming to Taylor-Mesilla Historic Site\nOctober 26, 2026\nFiesta of Cultures celebrates New Mexico’s diversity at Coronado Historic Site\nSeptember 10, 2026\nNew leadership coming to northern New Mexico historic sites\nSeptember 10, 2026\nPublic Meetings\nAs a state entity, New Mexico Historic Sites meetings are open to the public.\nVIEW ALL MEETINGS\nNOV\n17\nMuseum of New Mexico Board of Regents Regular Meeting\nNovember 17, 2026 | 1:00 PM\nMuseum of International Folk Art\nPublic Notice\n© 2003-2026 New Mexico Department of Cultural Affairs\nPrivacy Policy\nCopyright Statement\nTerms And Conditions\nIPRA Requests\nAccessibility\nSocial Media Standards\nDoing Business With DCA\nState Of New Mexico\nDCA Employment Opportunities\nPowered by Real Time Solutions – Website Design & Document Management",
